1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,971
An elder sister came from the town to
visit her younger sister in the

2
00:00:03,991 --> 00:00:07,037
country. The elder was married to a
merchant in town,

3
00:00:07,057 --> 00:00:09,351
the younger to a peasant in the village.

4
00:00:09,371 --> 00:00:11,722
The sisters sat drinking tea and talking.

5
00:00:11,742 --> 00:00:15,714
The elder sister began to put on airs and
to praise her life in town:

6
00:00:15,734 --> 00:00:18,721
how spaciously and cleanly she lived and
went about,

7
00:00:18,741 --> 00:00:22,192
how she dressed her children, how sweetly
she ate and drank,

8
00:00:22,212 --> 00:00:25,200
and how she went to drives, strolls and
the theatre.

9
00:00:25,970 --> 00:00:27,716
The younger sister was hurt,

10
00:00:27,736 --> 00:00:32,132
and she began to run down the merchant’s
life and to raise up her own,

11
00:00:32,152 --> 00:00:33,015
the peasant’s.

12
00:00:33,785 --> 00:00:36,016
“I would not trade my way of living for
yours,”

13
00:00:36,336 --> 00:00:37,178
she said.

14
00:00:37,498 --> 00:00:39,695
“We may live plainly, but we know no fear.

15
00:00:39,715 --> 00:00:40,804
You live more neatly,

16
00:00:40,824 --> 00:00:44,024
but you either make a great deal in trade
or lose everything.

17
00:00:44,044 --> 00:00:46,874
And as the proverb says: loss is profit’s
big brother.

18
00:00:46,894 --> 00:00:48,352
It happens, too: rich today,

19
00:00:48,372 --> 00:00:51,308
and tomorrow you are going about under
people’s windows.

20
00:00:51,328 --> 00:00:54,475
But our peasant business is surer: a
peasant’s life is thin,

21
00:00:54,495 --> 00:00:55,267
but it is long.

22
00:00:55,287 --> 00:00:58,276
We shall never be rich, but we shall have
enough to eat.”

23
00:00:59,046 --> 00:01:01,438
The elder sister began to say:

24
00:01:02,208 --> 00:01:04,972
“Enough to eat, indeed — with the pigs and
the calves!

25
00:01:04,992 --> 00:01:06,364
No furnishings, no manners!

26
00:01:06,384 --> 00:01:09,148
However hard your husband works, you live
in the dung,

27
00:01:09,168 --> 00:01:12,086
and so you will die, and your children
will be the same.”

28
00:01:12,856 --> 00:01:13,968
“Well, what of it?”

29
00:01:14,288 --> 00:01:15,427
says the younger.

30
00:01:15,747 --> 00:01:17,206
“That is how it is with us.

31
00:01:17,226 --> 00:01:20,547
But we live on firm ground, we bow to no
one, we fear no one.

32
00:01:20,567 --> 00:01:24,382
And you in town live among temptations all
the time; today it is well,

33
00:01:24,402 --> 00:01:26,847
and tomorrow the unclean one turns up —
look,

34
00:01:26,867 --> 00:01:30,845
and he tempts your husband to cards, or to
drink, or to some pretty girl.

35
00:01:30,865 --> 00:01:32,379
And everything goes to dust.

36
00:01:32,399 --> 00:01:33,584
Does that not happen?”

37
00:01:34,354 --> 00:01:37,466
Pakhom, the master of the house, lay on
the stove,

38
00:01:37,486 --> 00:01:40,597
listening to what the women were
chattering about.

39
00:01:41,367 --> 00:01:42,364
“That is true,”

40
00:01:42,684 --> 00:01:43,493
he said,

41
00:01:43,813 --> 00:01:44,793
“the real truth.

42
00:01:44,813 --> 00:01:48,044
From childhood our kind turn her over,
Mother Earth,

43
00:01:48,064 --> 00:01:50,733
so foolishness does not get into our
heads.

44
00:01:50,753 --> 00:01:53,484
Only one trouble — there is too little
land!

45
00:01:53,504 --> 00:01:56,359
But if I had land enough, I would fear no
one,

46
00:01:56,379 --> 00:01:58,110
not even the devil himself!”

47
00:01:58,880 --> 00:02:02,869
The women finished their tea, chattered a
little more about clothes,

48
00:02:02,889 --> 00:02:05,581
cleared away the dishes and lay down to
sleep.

49
00:02:06,351 --> 00:02:10,347
But the devil was sitting behind the stove
and heard everything.

50
00:02:10,367 --> 00:02:14,928
He was pleased that the peasant’s wife had
led her husband into boasting:

51
00:02:14,948 --> 00:02:19,321
he boasts that if only he had land, not
even the devil could take him.

52
00:02:20,091 --> 00:02:21,240
“Very well,”

53
00:02:21,560 --> 00:02:22,423
he thinks,

54
00:02:22,743 --> 00:02:26,352
“let us have a contest, you and I; I will
give you plenty of land.

55
00:02:26,372 --> 00:02:27,837
With land I will take you.”

56
00:02:30,357 --> 00:02:33,178
A small lady of the manor lived near the
peasants.

57
00:02:33,198 --> 00:02:35,906
She had a hundred and twenty desyatinas of
land.

58
00:02:35,926 --> 00:02:39,941
She had lived peacefully with the peasants
before and had never wronged

59
00:02:39,961 --> 00:02:40,225
them.

60
00:02:40,245 --> 00:02:44,373
But a retired soldier hired himself to her
as steward and began to plague

61
00:02:44,393 --> 00:02:45,737
the peasants with fines.

62
00:02:45,757 --> 00:02:49,431
However careful Pakhom was, either a horse
strayed into the oats,

63
00:02:49,451 --> 00:02:51,363
or a cow wandered into the garden,

64
00:02:51,383 --> 00:02:53,636
or the calves got out into the meadows —

65
00:02:53,656 --> 00:02:55,682
and for everything there was a fine.

66
00:02:56,452 --> 00:02:59,581
Pakhom paid, and scolded his household and
beat them.

67
00:02:59,601 --> 00:03:03,919
And Pakhom took on a great deal of sin
because of that steward during the

68
00:03:03,939 --> 00:03:07,722
summer. He was even glad when the cattle
were back in the yard —

69
00:03:07,742 --> 00:03:11,465
though he was sorry for the fodder, at
least there was no fear.

70
00:03:12,235 --> 00:03:16,767
In winter a rumour went round that the
lady was selling her land and that

71
00:03:16,787 --> 00:03:20,572
the keeper of the inn on the highway was
arranging to buy it.

72
00:03:20,592 --> 00:03:22,879
When the peasants heard, they gasped.

73
00:03:23,199 --> 00:03:24,012
“Well,”

74
00:03:24,332 --> 00:03:25,219
they thought,

75
00:03:25,539 --> 00:03:27,183
“the land will go to the innkeeper,

76
00:03:27,203 --> 00:03:29,751
and he will torment us with fines worse
than the lady.

77
00:03:29,771 --> 00:03:32,795
We cannot live without this land; we are
all within her circle.”

78
00:03:33,115 --> 00:03:37,449
The peasants came to the lady as a commune
and began to ask her not to

79
00:03:37,469 --> 00:03:40,248
sell to the innkeeper but to give it to
them.

80
00:03:40,268 --> 00:03:41,865
They promised to pay more.

81
00:03:41,885 --> 00:03:42,860
The lady agreed.

82
00:03:42,880 --> 00:03:47,401
The peasants began to arrange to buy all
the land together, as a commune;

83
00:03:47,421 --> 00:03:50,138
they gathered once and twice in assemblies
—

84
00:03:50,158 --> 00:03:52,315
the matter would not come together.

85
00:03:52,335 --> 00:03:56,482
The unclean one was dividing them, and
they could not agree at all.

86
00:03:56,502 --> 00:03:59,219
And the peasants resolved to buy
separately,

87
00:03:59,239 --> 00:04:01,210
each as much as he could manage.

88
00:04:01,230 --> 00:04:03,200
The lady agreed to that as well.

89
00:04:03,220 --> 00:04:07,679
Pakhom heard that a neighbour had bought
twenty desyatinas from the lady

90
00:04:07,699 --> 00:04:11,846
and that she had let him pay half the
money over a number of years.

91
00:04:11,866 --> 00:04:13,090
Pakhom grew envious.

92
00:04:13,410 --> 00:04:15,079
“They will buy up all the land,”

93
00:04:15,399 --> 00:04:16,230
he thought,

94
00:04:16,550 --> 00:04:18,481
“and I shall be left with nothing.”

95
00:04:18,801 --> 00:04:21,538
He began to take counsel with his wife.

96
00:04:22,308 --> 00:04:24,703
“People are buying, so we must buy too,”

97
00:04:25,023 --> 00:04:25,832
he said,

98
00:04:26,152 --> 00:04:27,626
“about ten desyatinas.

99
00:04:27,646 --> 00:04:32,108
Otherwise we cannot live: the steward has
worn us out with fines.”

100
00:04:32,878 --> 00:04:34,703
They thought over how to buy.

101
00:04:34,723 --> 00:04:38,265
They had a hundred roubles put by, and
they sold a foal,

102
00:04:38,285 --> 00:04:42,272
and half their bees, and put their son out
as a hired labourer,

103
00:04:42,292 --> 00:04:45,135
and borrowed some more from a brother-in-
law,

104
00:04:45,155 --> 00:04:47,361
and so half the money was gathered.

105
00:04:48,131 --> 00:04:51,524
Pakhom gathered the money, picked out a
piece of land,

106
00:04:51,544 --> 00:04:56,010
fifteen desyatinas with a little wood, and
went to the lady to bargain.

107
00:04:56,030 --> 00:04:58,538
He bargained for the fifteen desyatinas,

108
00:04:58,558 --> 00:05:00,940
struck hands on it and gave a deposit.

109
00:05:00,960 --> 00:05:03,657
They went to town, signed the deed of
sale,

110
00:05:03,677 --> 00:05:07,828
he paid half the money and undertook to
pay the rest in two years.

111
00:05:08,598 --> 00:05:10,487
And Pakhom had land of his own.

112
00:05:10,507 --> 00:05:14,244
Pakhom borrowed seed and sowed the bought
land; it bore well.

113
00:05:14,264 --> 00:05:18,739
In one year he paid off his debt to both
the lady and his brother-in-law.

114
00:05:18,759 --> 00:05:22,804
And Pakhom became a landowner: he ploughed
and sowed his own land,

115
00:05:22,824 --> 00:05:24,405
mowed hay on his own land,

116
00:05:24,425 --> 00:05:28,346
cut stakes from his own land and fed his
cattle on his own land.

117
00:05:28,366 --> 00:05:31,733
When Pakhom drove out to plough his land,
his for ever,

118
00:05:31,753 --> 00:05:34,935
or came to look at the young shoots and
the meadows,

119
00:05:34,955 --> 00:05:36,660
he could not rejoice enough.

120
00:05:36,680 --> 00:05:39,862
The grass, it seemed to him, grew
differently there,

121
00:05:39,882 --> 00:05:43,311
and the flowers that bloomed on it were
quite different.

122
00:05:43,331 --> 00:05:47,375
Before, when he drove across this land, it
was land like any land,

123
00:05:47,395 --> 00:05:50,023
but now it had become quite a special
land.

124
00:05:52,543 --> 00:05:54,436
So Pakhom lived, and was glad.

125
00:05:54,456 --> 00:05:56,030
All would have been well,

126
00:05:56,050 --> 00:05:59,983
only the peasants began to trample
Pakhom’s grain and meadows.

127
00:06:00,003 --> 00:06:03,044
He asked them politely, but they would not
stop:

128
00:06:03,064 --> 00:06:06,041
now the herdsmen let the cows into the
meadows,

129
00:06:06,061 --> 00:06:09,739
now the horses came from the night pasture
onto the grain.

130
00:06:09,759 --> 00:06:14,012
And Pakhom drove them off, and forgave
them, and did not go to law;

131
00:06:14,032 --> 00:06:18,411
then he grew weary of it and began to
complain to the district court.

132
00:06:18,431 --> 00:06:22,939
And he knew that the peasants did it from
lack of room, not on purpose,

133
00:06:22,959 --> 00:06:23,895
but he thought:

134
00:06:24,215 --> 00:06:27,882
“I cannot let it pass, or they will
trample everything down.

135
00:06:27,902 --> 00:06:29,725
They must be taught a lesson.”

136
00:06:30,495 --> 00:06:34,442
He taught them by law once, he taught them
twice; one was fined,

137
00:06:34,462 --> 00:06:35,247
then another.

138
00:06:35,267 --> 00:06:39,028
The peasant neighbours began to bear a
grudge against Pakhom;

139
00:06:39,048 --> 00:06:41,693
now and then they even trampled on
purpose.

140
00:06:41,713 --> 00:06:46,031
Someone got into the little wood one night
and cut down ten lime-trees

141
00:06:46,051 --> 00:06:50,432
for bast. Pakhom drove through the wood —
look, something showed white.

142
00:06:50,452 --> 00:06:54,956
He came up — the stripped poles lay thrown
about and the stumps stuck up.

143
00:06:54,976 --> 00:06:59,109
If only he had cut the ones at the edge of
the thicket and left one

144
00:06:59,129 --> 00:07:02,827
standing, but no, the villain had cleared
them all in a row.

145
00:07:02,847 --> 00:07:04,067
Pakhom grew furious.

146
00:07:04,387 --> 00:07:05,041
“Ah,”

147
00:07:05,361 --> 00:07:06,191
he thought,

148
00:07:06,511 --> 00:07:10,909
“if only I could find out who did this; I
would take my revenge on him.”

149
00:07:11,229 --> 00:07:13,516
He thought and thought who it was:

150
00:07:13,836 --> 00:07:15,246
“There is no one else,”

151
00:07:15,566 --> 00:07:16,397
he thought,

152
00:07:16,717 --> 00:07:17,741
“but Semka.”

153
00:07:18,061 --> 00:07:22,533
He went to Semka’s yard to search; he
found nothing, they only quarrelled.

154
00:07:22,553 --> 00:07:26,236
And Pakhom became still more certain that
Semyon had done it.

155
00:07:26,256 --> 00:07:27,692
He submitted a petition.

156
00:07:27,712 --> 00:07:29,392
They were summoned to court.

157
00:07:29,412 --> 00:07:33,884
They judged and judged — and acquitted the
peasant: there was no evidence.

158
00:07:33,904 --> 00:07:35,766
Pakhom was still more offended;

159
00:07:35,786 --> 00:07:38,740
he quarrelled with the elder and with the
judges.

160
00:07:39,510 --> 00:07:40,074
“You,”

161
00:07:40,394 --> 00:07:41,204
he said,

162
00:07:41,524 --> 00:07:42,607
“side with thieves.

163
00:07:42,627 --> 00:07:46,553
If you lived by the truth yourselves, you
would not acquit thieves.”

164
00:07:47,323 --> 00:07:50,953
Pakhom quarrelled with the judges and with
his neighbours.

165
00:07:50,973 --> 00:07:54,100
They even began to threaten him with the
red cock.

166
00:07:54,120 --> 00:07:58,442
And Pakhom now had more room on the land,
but less room among people.

167
00:07:59,212 --> 00:08:03,590
And at that time a rumour went round that
people were going off to new

168
00:08:03,610 --> 00:08:05,287
places. And Pakhom thought:

169
00:08:05,607 --> 00:08:08,542
“For myself there is no need to leave my
land,

170
00:08:08,562 --> 00:08:12,204
but if some of our people went, we should
have more room.

171
00:08:12,224 --> 00:08:15,801
I would take their land for myself and add
it to my own;

172
00:08:15,821 --> 00:08:17,150
life would be better.

173
00:08:17,170 --> 00:08:19,013
As it is, it is all cramped.”

174
00:08:19,783 --> 00:08:23,303
Once Pakhom was sitting at home, and a
peasant passing by came in.

175
00:08:23,323 --> 00:08:27,058
They let the peasant stay the night, fed
him, and got talking — where,

176
00:08:27,078 --> 00:08:29,096
they asked, was God bringing him from?

177
00:08:29,116 --> 00:08:32,850
The peasant said that he was coming from
below, from beyond the Volga,

178
00:08:32,870 --> 00:08:34,245
where he had been working.

179
00:08:34,265 --> 00:08:37,946
Word by word, the peasant told how people
were going there to settle.

180
00:08:37,966 --> 00:08:40,252
He told how their people had settled
there,

181
00:08:40,272 --> 00:08:42,076
had been entered into the commune,

182
00:08:42,096 --> 00:08:44,865
and ten desyatinas a head had been cut out
for them.

183
00:08:45,635 --> 00:08:47,454
“And the land is such,”

184
00:08:47,774 --> 00:08:48,583
he said,

185
00:08:48,903 --> 00:08:50,241
“that when they sowed rye,

186
00:08:50,261 --> 00:08:52,905
the straw grew so high you could not see
the horse,

187
00:08:52,925 --> 00:08:55,986
and so thick that five handfuls made a
sheaf. One peasant,”

188
00:08:56,306 --> 00:08:57,116
he said,

189
00:08:57,436 --> 00:08:59,814
“came quite poor, with nothing but his
hands,

190
00:08:59,834 --> 00:09:01,946
and now he has six horses and two cows.”

191
00:09:02,716 --> 00:09:05,134
Pakhom’s heart took fire. He thought:

192
00:09:05,454 --> 00:09:09,006
“Why suffer here in the crowding when one
can live well?

193
00:09:09,026 --> 00:09:11,494
I will sell the land and the yard here;

194
00:09:11,514 --> 00:09:15,195
there I will build and set up a whole farm
with the money.

195
00:09:15,215 --> 00:09:18,448
And here in this crowding there is nothing
but sin.

196
00:09:18,468 --> 00:09:21,255
Only I must find everything out for
myself.”

197
00:09:22,025 --> 00:09:24,443
He got ready for the summer and went.

198
00:09:24,463 --> 00:09:27,935
He sailed down the Volga by steamer as far
as Samara,

199
00:09:27,955 --> 00:09:30,439
then went four hundred versts on foot.

200
00:09:30,459 --> 00:09:31,823
He reached the place.

201
00:09:31,843 --> 00:09:33,932
It was all exactly as described.

202
00:09:33,952 --> 00:09:35,843
The peasants live spaciously,

203
00:09:35,863 --> 00:09:39,071
ten desyatinas of land are cut out for
each soul,

204
00:09:39,091 --> 00:09:42,366
and they are glad to take people into the
commune.

205
00:09:42,386 --> 00:09:46,847
And if anyone has a little money, he can
buy, besides his allotment,

206
00:09:46,867 --> 00:09:49,285
as much as he likes to hold for ever,

207
00:09:49,305 --> 00:09:51,921
at three roubles for the very best land;

208
00:09:51,941 --> 00:09:54,161
as much as you like can be bought!

209
00:09:54,931 --> 00:09:56,796
Pakhom found out everything,

210
00:09:56,816 --> 00:10:00,364
returned home by autumn and began to sell
everything.

211
00:10:00,384 --> 00:10:04,739
He sold his land at a profit, sold his
yard, sold all his cattle,

212
00:10:04,759 --> 00:10:07,768
withdrew from the commune, waited for
spring,

213
00:10:07,788 --> 00:10:10,932
and set out with his family for the new
places.

214
00:10:13,452 --> 00:10:18,258
Pakhom arrived at the new places with his
family and was entered into a

215
00:10:18,278 --> 00:10:19,753
large village commune.

216
00:10:19,773 --> 00:10:24,035
He treated the elders to drink and put all
his papers in order.

217
00:10:24,055 --> 00:10:27,909
They accepted Pakhom and cut out for him,
for five souls,

218
00:10:27,929 --> 00:10:32,667
fifty desyatinas of allotment land in
various fields, besides pasture.

219
00:10:32,687 --> 00:10:35,454
Pakhom built a house and acquired cattle.

220
00:10:35,474 --> 00:10:39,600
He now had three times as much land per
soul alone as before.

221
00:10:39,620 --> 00:10:41,231
And the land bore grain.

222
00:10:41,251 --> 00:10:44,494
Life was ten times better than in the old
place.

223
00:10:44,514 --> 00:10:47,144
He had plenty of ploughland and fodder.

224
00:10:47,164 --> 00:10:49,931
He could keep as many cattle as he liked.

225
00:10:50,701 --> 00:10:54,406
At first, while he was building and
settling in, Pakhom was pleased,

226
00:10:54,426 --> 00:10:57,967
but when he had settled in, even this land
began to seem cramped.

227
00:10:57,987 --> 00:11:01,911
In the first year Pakhom sowed wheat on
his allotment — it came up well.

228
00:11:01,931 --> 00:11:05,746
He took a liking to sowing wheat, but
there was little allotment land.

229
00:11:05,766 --> 00:11:07,718
And what there was was not suitable.

230
00:11:07,738 --> 00:11:10,567
Wheat is sown there on feather-grass or
fallow land.

231
00:11:10,587 --> 00:11:14,566
They sow it for a year or two and then
leave it until feather-grass grows

232
00:11:14,586 --> 00:11:15,333
over it again.

233
00:11:15,353 --> 00:11:19,222
And there are many who want such land, and
there is not enough for all.

234
00:11:19,242 --> 00:11:20,921
There are disputes over it too;

235
00:11:20,941 --> 00:11:23,331
the richer people want to sow it
themselves,

236
00:11:23,351 --> 00:11:26,070
and the poor give it to merchants for
their taxes.

237
00:11:26,090 --> 00:11:27,495
Pakhom wanted to sow more.

238
00:11:27,515 --> 00:11:30,946
The next year he went to a merchant and
bought land for a year.

239
00:11:30,966 --> 00:11:34,397
He sowed more — it bore well; but it was
far from the village —

240
00:11:34,417 --> 00:11:36,205
he had to haul it fifteen versts.

241
00:11:36,225 --> 00:11:40,204
He saw that the merchant-peasants round
about lived on separate farms and

242
00:11:40,224 --> 00:11:41,190
were growing rich.

243
00:11:41,510 --> 00:11:42,807
“That is the thing,”

244
00:11:43,127 --> 00:11:44,296
thought Pakhom,

245
00:11:44,616 --> 00:11:48,258
“if only I too could buy land for ever and
build a farm.

246
00:11:48,278 --> 00:11:51,332
Then everything would be within my own
circle.”

247
00:11:51,652 --> 00:11:55,266
And Pakhom began to think how to buy land
for ever.

248
00:11:56,036 --> 00:11:57,960
So Pakhom lived for three years.

249
00:11:57,980 --> 00:11:59,844
He rented land and sowed wheat.

250
00:11:59,864 --> 00:12:03,976
The years were good, and the wheat bore
well, and he had idle money.

251
00:12:03,996 --> 00:12:05,434
One could live and live,

252
00:12:05,454 --> 00:12:09,748
but Pakhom grew bored with buying land
every year from other people and

253
00:12:09,768 --> 00:12:13,759
fussing over land: wherever there was a
nice little piece of land,

254
00:12:13,779 --> 00:12:16,675
peasants would fly down at once and take
it all;

255
00:12:16,695 --> 00:12:20,017
if he was not in time to buy, he had
nothing to sow on.

256
00:12:20,037 --> 00:12:24,392
And in the third year he bought a pasture
from the peasants jointly with

257
00:12:24,412 --> 00:12:27,126
a merchant; and they had already ploughed
it,

258
00:12:27,146 --> 00:12:30,225
but the peasants went to law, so the work
was lost.

259
00:12:30,545 --> 00:12:32,501
“If only I had land of my own,”

260
00:12:32,821 --> 00:12:33,651
he thought,

261
00:12:33,971 --> 00:12:36,962
“I should bow to no one, and there would
be no sin.”

262
00:12:37,732 --> 00:12:41,159
And Pakhom began to inquire where to buy
land for ever.

263
00:12:41,179 --> 00:12:42,977
And he came across a peasant.

264
00:12:42,997 --> 00:12:46,487
Five hundred desyatinas had been bought by
this peasant,

265
00:12:46,507 --> 00:12:49,495
but he had gone to ruin and was selling
cheaply.

266
00:12:49,515 --> 00:12:51,563
Pakhom began to bargain with him.

267
00:12:51,583 --> 00:12:55,261
They talked and talked — agreed on fifteen
hundred roubles,

268
00:12:55,281 --> 00:12:57,016
with half the money to wait.

269
00:12:57,036 --> 00:12:58,708
They had almost settled it,

270
00:12:58,728 --> 00:13:03,033
when a passing merchant happened to stop
at Pakhom’s yard to feed his

271
00:13:03,053 --> 00:13:05,164
horses. They drank tea and talked.

272
00:13:05,184 --> 00:13:08,862
The merchant said that he was coming from
the far Bashkirs.

273
00:13:08,882 --> 00:13:09,802
There, he said,

274
00:13:09,822 --> 00:13:13,876
he had bought five thousand desyatinas of
land from the Bashkirs.

275
00:13:13,896 --> 00:13:16,759
And it had cost altogether a thousand
roubles.

276
00:13:16,779 --> 00:13:18,639
Pakhom began to ask questions.

277
00:13:18,659 --> 00:13:20,018
The merchant told him.

278
00:13:20,788 --> 00:13:21,815
“I only,”

279
00:13:22,135 --> 00:13:22,944
he said,

280
00:13:23,264 --> 00:13:24,426
“pleased the old men.

281
00:13:24,446 --> 00:13:28,027
I gave away about a hundred roubles’ worth
of robes and carpets,

282
00:13:28,047 --> 00:13:31,009
and a chest of tea, and gave wine to those
who drink.

283
00:13:31,029 --> 00:13:33,541
And I took it at twenty kopecks a
desyatina.”

284
00:13:33,861 --> 00:13:35,576
He showed the deed of sale.

285
00:13:35,896 --> 00:13:36,850
“The land,”

286
00:13:37,170 --> 00:13:37,979
he said,

287
00:13:38,299 --> 00:13:41,147
“is along a river, and the steppe is all
feather-grass.”

288
00:13:41,917 --> 00:13:44,409
Pakhom began to ask how and what.

289
00:13:45,179 --> 00:13:46,133
“The land,”

290
00:13:46,453 --> 00:13:47,642
said the merchant,

291
00:13:47,962 --> 00:13:50,673
“you could not walk round in a year: it is
all Bashkir.

292
00:13:50,693 --> 00:13:52,560
And the people are simple, like sheep.

293
00:13:52,580 --> 00:13:54,348
You can take it almost for nothing.”

294
00:13:55,118 --> 00:13:55,775
“Well,”

295
00:13:56,095 --> 00:13:57,264
thought Pakhom,

296
00:13:57,584 --> 00:14:02,097
“why should I buy five hundred desyatinas
for my thousand roubles and

297
00:14:02,117 --> 00:14:04,133
take a debt on my neck besides?

298
00:14:04,153 --> 00:14:08,535
And there, for a thousand roubles, what
shall I get into my hands!”

299
00:14:11,055 --> 00:14:13,110
Pakhom asked how to get there and,

300
00:14:13,130 --> 00:14:17,077
as soon as he had seen the merchant off,
got ready to go himself.

301
00:14:17,097 --> 00:14:21,350
He left the house to his wife, got ready
with a hired man and set out.

302
00:14:21,370 --> 00:14:24,890
They stopped in town, bought a chest of
tea, gifts, wine —

303
00:14:24,910 --> 00:14:27,087
everything as the merchant had said.

304
00:14:27,107 --> 00:14:30,810
They travelled and travelled and covered
five hundred versts.

305
00:14:30,830 --> 00:14:34,045
On the seventh day they came to a Bashkir
encampment.

306
00:14:34,065 --> 00:14:36,242
It was all as the merchant had said.

307
00:14:36,262 --> 00:14:39,599
They all live in the steppe, by a river,
in felt tents.

308
00:14:39,619 --> 00:14:42,040
They do not plough and do not eat bread.

309
00:14:42,060 --> 00:14:45,214
And in the steppe cattle graze, and horses
in herds.

310
00:14:45,234 --> 00:14:47,350
Foals are tied up behind the tents,

311
00:14:47,370 --> 00:14:50,096
and twice a day the mares are driven to
them;

312
00:14:50,116 --> 00:14:53,087
they milk the mares and make kumis from
the milk.

313
00:14:53,107 --> 00:14:55,650
The women churn the kumis and make cheese,

314
00:14:55,670 --> 00:14:58,824
and the men know nothing but drinking
kumis and tea,

315
00:14:58,844 --> 00:15:00,960
eating mutton and playing on pipes.

316
00:15:00,980 --> 00:15:04,683
They are all sleek and cheerful, and make
holiday all summer.

317
00:15:04,703 --> 00:15:08,406
They are quite an unlettered people, and
do not know Russian,

318
00:15:08,426 --> 00:15:09,749
but they are friendly.

319
00:15:10,519 --> 00:15:12,212
As soon as they saw Pakhom,

320
00:15:12,232 --> 00:15:16,145
the Bashkirs came out of their tents and
surrounded the guest.

321
00:15:16,165 --> 00:15:17,730
An interpreter was found.

322
00:15:17,750 --> 00:15:20,521
Pakhom told him that he had come about
land.

323
00:15:20,541 --> 00:15:24,771
The Bashkirs were delighted, took Pakhom,
led him into a fine tent,

324
00:15:24,791 --> 00:15:28,260
sat him on carpets, put down-filled
cushions under him,

325
00:15:28,280 --> 00:15:31,876
sat around and began to entertain him with
tea and kumis.

326
00:15:31,896 --> 00:15:34,667
They slaughtered a sheep and fed him
mutton.

327
00:15:34,687 --> 00:15:38,917
Pakhom took the gifts out of the cart and
began to give them to the

328
00:15:38,937 --> 00:15:43,167
Bashkirs. Pakhom gave the Bashkirs
presents and shared out the tea.

329
00:15:43,187 --> 00:15:44,943
The Bashkirs were delighted.

330
00:15:44,963 --> 00:15:47,861
They chattered and chattered among
themselves,

331
00:15:47,881 --> 00:15:50,081
then told the interpreter to speak.

332
00:15:50,851 --> 00:15:52,347
“They tell me to say to you,”

333
00:15:52,667 --> 00:15:54,121
said the interpreter,

334
00:15:54,441 --> 00:15:57,888
“that they have taken a liking to you and
that it is our custom to do a

335
00:15:57,908 --> 00:16:00,672
guest every kindness and to repay presents
with presents.

336
00:16:00,692 --> 00:16:03,651
You have given us gifts; now say what of
ours you would like,

337
00:16:03,671 --> 00:16:04,921
so that we may repay you.”

338
00:16:05,691 --> 00:16:07,713
“What I liked most of all among you,”

339
00:16:08,033 --> 00:16:09,097
said Pakhom,

340
00:16:09,417 --> 00:16:11,021
“is the land. With us,”

341
00:16:11,341 --> 00:16:12,150
he said,

342
00:16:12,470 --> 00:16:15,035
“land is cramped, and the land is worn
out,

343
00:16:15,055 --> 00:16:18,460
while you have a great deal of land and
the land is good.

344
00:16:18,480 --> 00:16:19,903
I have never seen such.”

345
00:16:20,673 --> 00:16:22,500
The interpreter passed it on.

346
00:16:22,520 --> 00:16:24,475
The Bashkirs talked and talked.

347
00:16:24,495 --> 00:16:27,087
Pakhom did not understand what they said,

348
00:16:27,107 --> 00:16:31,228
but he saw that they were cheerful,
shouting something, laughing.

349
00:16:31,248 --> 00:16:35,623
Then they fell silent and looked at
Pakhom, and the interpreter said:

350
00:16:36,393 --> 00:16:37,889
“They tell me to say to you,”

351
00:16:38,209 --> 00:16:39,018
he said,

352
00:16:39,338 --> 00:16:42,793
“that in return for your kindness they are
glad to give you as much land

353
00:16:42,813 --> 00:16:43,373
as you want.

354
00:16:43,393 --> 00:16:46,703
Just show it with your hand — whichever
you show — it will be yours.”

355
00:16:47,473 --> 00:16:51,037
They talked some more and began to argue
about something.

356
00:16:51,057 --> 00:16:53,930
And Pakhom asked what they were arguing
about.

357
00:16:53,950 --> 00:16:55,502
And the interpreter said:

358
00:16:56,272 --> 00:16:58,899
“Some say that they must ask the elder
about the land,

359
00:16:58,919 --> 00:17:00,810
and that without him it cannot be done.

360
00:17:00,830 --> 00:17:03,163
And others say that it can be done without
him.”

361
00:17:05,683 --> 00:17:10,749
The Bashkirs were arguing, when suddenly a
man in a fox-fur cap came up.

362
00:17:10,769 --> 00:17:12,585
All fell silent and stood.

363
00:17:12,605 --> 00:17:14,351
And the interpreter said:

364
00:17:15,121 --> 00:17:16,862
“That is the elder himself.”

365
00:17:17,632 --> 00:17:22,021
At once Pakhom took out his best robe and
presented it to the elder,

366
00:17:22,041 --> 00:17:23,837
and five pounds more of tea.

367
00:17:23,857 --> 00:17:27,597
The elder accepted it and sat down in the
place of honour.

368
00:17:27,617 --> 00:17:31,034
And at once the Bashkirs began to tell him
something.

369
00:17:31,054 --> 00:17:35,637
The elder listened and listened, nodded
his head for them to be silent,

370
00:17:35,657 --> 00:17:38,231
and began to speak to Pakhom in Russian.

371
00:17:39,001 --> 00:17:39,885
“Well,”

372
00:17:40,205 --> 00:17:41,015
he said,

373
00:17:41,335 --> 00:17:42,252
“it can be done.

374
00:17:42,272 --> 00:17:43,424
Take where you like.

375
00:17:43,444 --> 00:17:44,888
There is plenty of land.”

376
00:17:45,658 --> 00:17:47,649
“How can I take as much as I want,”

377
00:17:47,969 --> 00:17:49,157
thinks Pakhom.

378
00:17:49,477 --> 00:17:51,422
“It must somehow be made firm.

379
00:17:51,442 --> 00:17:55,351
Otherwise they will say it is yours, and
then take it away.”

380
00:17:56,121 --> 00:17:57,016
“We thank you,”

381
00:17:57,336 --> 00:17:58,145
he said,

382
00:17:58,465 --> 00:17:59,661
“for your kind word.

383
00:17:59,681 --> 00:18:03,068
You have a great deal of land, and I need
only a little.

384
00:18:03,088 --> 00:18:05,440
I only want to know which will be mine.

385
00:18:05,460 --> 00:18:08,602
It must somehow be measured off and made
fast to me.

386
00:18:08,622 --> 00:18:10,914
For life and death are in God’s power.

387
00:18:10,934 --> 00:18:14,016
You, good people, give it, but when the
time comes,

388
00:18:14,036 --> 00:18:15,962
your children may take it away.”

389
00:18:16,732 --> 00:18:17,731
“You are right,”

390
00:18:18,051 --> 00:18:19,129
said the elder,

391
00:18:19,449 --> 00:18:21,250
“it can be made fast.”

392
00:18:22,020 --> 00:18:23,709
Pakhom began to say:

393
00:18:24,479 --> 00:18:27,002
“I heard that a merchant was here with
you.

394
00:18:27,022 --> 00:18:30,727
You gave him a bit of land as well and
made out a deed of sale;

395
00:18:30,747 --> 00:18:32,146
I should like the same.”

396
00:18:32,916 --> 00:18:35,332
The elder understood everything.

397
00:18:36,102 --> 00:18:37,623
“All that can be done,”

398
00:18:37,943 --> 00:18:38,766
he said.

399
00:18:39,086 --> 00:18:42,765
“We have a clerk too, and we will drive to
town and put on all the seals.”

400
00:18:43,535 --> 00:18:45,142
“And what will the price be?”

401
00:18:45,462 --> 00:18:46,541
said Pakhom.

402
00:18:47,311 --> 00:18:50,609
“Our price is one: a thousand roubles a
day.”

403
00:18:51,379 --> 00:18:53,569
Pakhom did not understand.

404
00:18:54,339 --> 00:18:56,138
“What sort of measure is a day?

405
00:18:56,158 --> 00:18:58,544
How many desyatinas will there be in it?”

406
00:18:59,314 --> 00:19:01,608
“We do not know how to count that,”

407
00:19:01,928 --> 00:19:02,751
he said.

408
00:19:03,071 --> 00:19:06,138
“We sell by the day; as much as you can
walk round in a day,

409
00:19:06,158 --> 00:19:09,276
that is yours, and the price of a day is a
thousand roubles.”

410
00:19:10,046 --> 00:19:11,816
Pakhom was astonished.

411
00:19:12,586 --> 00:19:14,219
“But to walk round in a day,”

412
00:19:14,539 --> 00:19:15,348
he said,

413
00:19:15,668 --> 00:19:17,375
“there would be a great deal of land.”

414
00:19:18,145 --> 00:19:19,380
The elder laughed.

415
00:19:20,150 --> 00:19:21,255
“All yours!”

416
00:19:21,575 --> 00:19:22,398
he said.

417
00:19:22,718 --> 00:19:23,702
“Only one condition:

418
00:19:23,722 --> 00:19:27,267
if you do not come back in the day to the
place from which you started,

419
00:19:27,287 --> 00:19:28,271
your money is lost.”

420
00:19:29,041 --> 00:19:29,824
“And how,”

421
00:19:30,144 --> 00:19:31,208
said Pakhom,

422
00:19:31,528 --> 00:19:33,814
“is the place where I walk to be marked?”

423
00:19:34,584 --> 00:19:37,353
“We will stand at the place you choose, we
will stand there,

424
00:19:37,373 --> 00:19:40,793
and you go and make a circle; and take a
spade with you and, where needed,

425
00:19:40,813 --> 00:19:43,396
make marks, dig a hole at the corners, lay
turves in it,

426
00:19:43,416 --> 00:19:45,627
then we will go with a plough from hole to
hole.

427
00:19:45,647 --> 00:19:47,022
Take whatever circle you like,

428
00:19:47,042 --> 00:19:50,229
only before the sun sets come back to the
place from which you began.

429
00:19:50,249 --> 00:19:52,321
Whatever you walk round, all of it is
yours.”

430
00:19:53,091 --> 00:19:54,453
Pakhom was delighted.

431
00:19:54,473 --> 00:19:56,494
They resolved to set out early.

432
00:19:56,514 --> 00:20:01,300
They talked, drank more kumis, ate mutton,
drank more tea; night came on.

433
00:20:01,320 --> 00:20:04,460
They laid Pakhom down to sleep on a
feather bed,

434
00:20:04,480 --> 00:20:06,237
and the Bashkirs dispersed.

435
00:20:06,257 --> 00:20:10,977
They promised to gather tomorrow at dawn
and to ride to the place before

436
00:20:10,997 --> 00:20:11,504
sunrise.

437
00:20:14,024 --> 00:20:17,520
Pakhom lay down on the feather beds, and
could not sleep,

438
00:20:17,540 --> 00:20:19,618
thinking all the time of the land.

439
00:20:19,938 --> 00:20:21,723
“I shall seize a great tract,”

440
00:20:22,043 --> 00:20:22,956
he thinks.

441
00:20:23,276 --> 00:20:26,115
“I shall walk round fifty versts in the
day.

442
00:20:26,135 --> 00:20:28,844
A day now is like a year; in fifty versts,

443
00:20:28,864 --> 00:20:30,988
what a lot of land there will be.

444
00:20:31,008 --> 00:20:34,042
The worse part I shall sell or let to
peasants,

445
00:20:34,062 --> 00:20:37,226
and the best I shall choose and settle on
myself.

446
00:20:37,246 --> 00:20:40,735
I shall get two ploughs with oxen, hire
two labourers;

447
00:20:40,755 --> 00:20:43,139
I shall plough some fifty desyatinas,

448
00:20:43,159 --> 00:20:45,738
and on the rest I shall pasture cattle.”

449
00:20:46,508 --> 00:20:48,228
Pakhom did not sleep all night.

450
00:20:48,248 --> 00:20:50,080
Only before dawn did he doze off.

451
00:20:50,100 --> 00:20:52,549
Hardly had he dozed off when he saw a
dream.

452
00:20:52,569 --> 00:20:55,692
He sees that he is lying, as it were, in
this very tent,

453
00:20:55,712 --> 00:20:57,713
and hears someone guffawing outside.

454
00:20:57,733 --> 00:21:01,361
And he wanted to see who it was that was
laughing, and he got up,

455
00:21:01,381 --> 00:21:03,100
went out of the tent and sees —

456
00:21:03,120 --> 00:21:06,299
the very same Bashkir elder sitting in
front of the tent,

457
00:21:06,319 --> 00:21:09,611
clutching his belly with both hands,
rocking with laughter,

458
00:21:09,631 --> 00:21:10,901
guffawing at something.

459
00:21:10,921 --> 00:21:12,080
He went up and asked:

460
00:21:12,400 --> 00:21:13,557
“What are you laughing at?”

461
00:21:13,877 --> 00:21:18,139
And he sees that it is not the Bashkir
elder but the merchant of the

462
00:21:18,159 --> 00:21:22,295
other day, who had stopped at their place
and told about the land.

463
00:21:22,315 --> 00:21:24,499
And he had only asked the merchant:

464
00:21:24,819 --> 00:21:26,249
“Have you been here long?”

465
00:21:26,569 --> 00:21:28,536
— and it was no longer the merchant,

466
00:21:28,556 --> 00:21:32,345
but that very peasant who had come from
below, back at the old place.

467
00:21:32,365 --> 00:21:36,265
And Pakhom sees that it is not even the
peasant, but the devil himself,

468
00:21:36,285 --> 00:21:39,411
with horns and hooves, sitting and roaring
with laughter,

469
00:21:39,431 --> 00:21:42,724
and before him lies a man barefoot, in a
shirt and trousers.

470
00:21:42,744 --> 00:21:46,201
And Pakhom, as it were, looked more
closely: what man was this?

471
00:21:46,221 --> 00:21:49,293
And he sees that the man is dead and that
it is himself.

472
00:21:49,313 --> 00:21:51,777
Pakhom was horrified and woke up. He woke
up.

473
00:21:52,097 --> 00:21:53,761
“What things one dreams,”

474
00:21:54,081 --> 00:21:55,869
he thinks. He looked around;

475
00:21:55,889 --> 00:21:59,614
through the open door he sees it is
already growing white,

476
00:21:59,634 --> 00:22:00,776
dawn is beginning.

477
00:22:01,096 --> 00:22:02,635
“I must wake the people,”

478
00:22:02,955 --> 00:22:03,819
he thinks,

479
00:22:04,139 --> 00:22:05,510
“it is time to go.”

480
00:22:05,830 --> 00:22:10,155
Pakhom got up, woke the hired man in the
cart, told him to harness,

481
00:22:10,175 --> 00:22:12,101
and went to wake the Bashkirs.

482
00:22:12,871 --> 00:22:13,838
“It is time,”

483
00:22:14,158 --> 00:22:14,968
he said,

484
00:22:15,288 --> 00:22:17,384
“to drive out to the steppe and measure.”

485
00:22:18,154 --> 00:22:21,644
The Bashkirs got up, all gathered, and the
elder came.

486
00:22:21,664 --> 00:22:26,455
The Bashkirs began to drink kumis again
and wanted to treat Pakhom to tea,

487
00:22:26,475 --> 00:22:27,885
but he would not wait.

488
00:22:28,655 --> 00:22:30,424
“If we are going, let us go,”

489
00:22:30,744 --> 00:22:31,553
he said,

490
00:22:31,873 --> 00:22:32,877
“it is time.”

491
00:22:35,397 --> 00:22:39,347
The Bashkirs gathered, mounted — some on
horseback, some in carts —

492
00:22:39,367 --> 00:22:43,673
and set out. And Pakhom with his hired man
went in their own little cart,

493
00:22:43,693 --> 00:22:45,273
and took a spade with them.

494
00:22:45,293 --> 00:22:47,821
They came to the steppe; dawn was
breaking.

495
00:22:47,841 --> 00:22:51,080
They drove up onto a small mound, in
Bashkir a shikhan.

496
00:22:51,100 --> 00:22:53,036
They climbed down from the carts,

497
00:22:53,056 --> 00:22:55,702
got off their horses and gathered in a
group.

498
00:22:55,722 --> 00:22:58,843
The elder came up to Pakhom and showed
with his hand.

499
00:22:59,613 --> 00:23:00,473
“Here,”

500
00:23:00,793 --> 00:23:01,602
he said,

501
00:23:01,922 --> 00:23:05,334
“all ours, as far as the eye can see.
Choose any.”

502
00:23:06,104 --> 00:23:10,670
Pakhom’s eyes kindled: the land was all
feather-grass, flat as a palm,

503
00:23:10,690 --> 00:23:13,945
black as poppy-seed, and where there was a
hollow,

504
00:23:13,965 --> 00:23:16,828
there were mixed grasses, grass breast-
high.

505
00:23:17,598 --> 00:23:21,378
The elder took off his fox-fur cap and set
it on the ground.

506
00:23:22,148 --> 00:23:23,008
“Here,”

507
00:23:23,328 --> 00:23:24,138
he said,

508
00:23:24,458 --> 00:23:25,449
“will be the mark.

509
00:23:25,469 --> 00:23:27,078
Go from here, come back here.

510
00:23:27,098 --> 00:23:29,269
Whatever you walk round will be yours.”

511
00:23:30,039 --> 00:23:33,760
Pakhom took out his money, put it on the
cap, took off his coat,

512
00:23:33,780 --> 00:23:37,618
stayed in his short jacket, tied his sash
tighter under his belly,

513
00:23:37,638 --> 00:23:41,769
pulled himself together; he put a little
bag of bread inside his shirt,

514
00:23:41,789 --> 00:23:45,335
tied the flask of water to his sash,
pulled up his boot-tops,

515
00:23:45,355 --> 00:23:48,491
took the spade from the hired man and got
ready to go.

516
00:23:48,511 --> 00:23:52,817
He thought and thought which way to take —
everywhere was good. He thinks:

517
00:23:53,137 --> 00:23:56,501
“It is all the same: I shall go toward the
sunrise.”

518
00:23:56,821 --> 00:23:59,212
He stood facing the sun, limbered up,

519
00:23:59,232 --> 00:24:02,796
waiting for it to show itself over the
edge. He thinks:

520
00:24:03,116 --> 00:24:05,139
“I shall let no time slip by.

521
00:24:05,159 --> 00:24:07,535
In the cool it is easier to walk.”

522
00:24:07,855 --> 00:24:10,558
As soon as the sun flashed out over the
edge,

523
00:24:10,578 --> 00:24:14,794
Pakhom threw the spade onto his shoulder
and went out into the steppe.

524
00:24:15,564 --> 00:24:17,874
Pakhom went neither slowly nor fast.

525
00:24:17,894 --> 00:24:20,140
He went off about a verst; stopped,

526
00:24:20,160 --> 00:24:22,858
dug a hole and laid turves one on another,

527
00:24:22,878 --> 00:24:25,965
so that it would be more noticeable. He
went on.

528
00:24:25,985 --> 00:24:29,137
He began to limber up, began to quicken
his step.

529
00:24:29,157 --> 00:24:31,532
He went farther and dug another hole.

530
00:24:32,302 --> 00:24:33,434
Pakhom looked back.

531
00:24:33,454 --> 00:24:37,376
In the sun the shikhan was plain to see,
and the people standing,

532
00:24:37,396 --> 00:24:40,226
and the tires on the wheels of the carts
shone.

533
00:24:40,246 --> 00:24:42,894
Pakhom guessed that he had gone five
versts.

534
00:24:42,914 --> 00:24:47,139
He began to get warm, took off his jacket,
threw it over his shoulder,

535
00:24:47,159 --> 00:24:50,232
went on. He went another five versts. It
grew warm.

536
00:24:50,252 --> 00:24:53,749
He glanced at the sun — it was already
time for breakfast.

537
00:24:54,519 --> 00:24:55,984
“One stretch is done,”

538
00:24:56,304 --> 00:24:57,492
thinks Pakhom.

539
00:24:57,812 --> 00:25:01,285
“And there are four in a day; it is too
early to turn.

540
00:25:01,305 --> 00:25:03,291
Just let me take off my boots.”

541
00:25:03,611 --> 00:25:08,024
He sat down, took off his boots, tucked
them in his sash, went on.

542
00:25:08,044 --> 00:25:10,106
Walking became easy. He thinks:

543
00:25:10,426 --> 00:25:15,023
“Let me go another five versts or so, then
I shall begin to turn left.

544
00:25:15,043 --> 00:25:18,914
The place is very good, it would be a pity
to leave it out.

545
00:25:18,934 --> 00:25:20,563
The farther, the better.”

546
00:25:20,883 --> 00:25:22,487
He went on straight ahead.

547
00:25:22,507 --> 00:25:25,484
He looked back — the shikhan was barely
visible,

548
00:25:25,504 --> 00:25:28,232
and the people on it showed black like
ants,

549
00:25:28,252 --> 00:25:30,105
and something gleamed faintly.

550
00:25:30,875 --> 00:25:31,532
“Well,”

551
00:25:31,852 --> 00:25:33,065
thinks Pakhom,

552
00:25:33,385 --> 00:25:36,840
“I have taken enough in this direction; I
must turn.

553
00:25:36,860 --> 00:25:39,245
And I am in a sweat — I am thirsty.”

554
00:25:39,565 --> 00:25:43,734
He stopped, dug a bigger hole, laid
turves, untied the flask,

555
00:25:43,754 --> 00:25:45,794
drank and turned sharply left.

556
00:25:45,814 --> 00:25:49,640
He went and went; the grass became tall,
and it was hot.

557
00:25:50,410 --> 00:25:55,130
Pakhom began to tire; he looked at the sun
and saw it was just midday.

558
00:25:55,450 --> 00:25:56,107
“Well,”

559
00:25:56,427 --> 00:25:57,290
he thinks,

560
00:25:57,610 --> 00:25:58,732
“I must rest.”

561
00:25:59,052 --> 00:26:00,717
Pakhom stopped and sat down.

562
00:26:00,737 --> 00:26:04,809
He ate a little bread with water, but did
not lie down: he thought —

563
00:26:04,829 --> 00:26:07,096
if you lie down, you will fall asleep.

564
00:26:07,116 --> 00:26:09,142
He sat a little while and went on.

565
00:26:09,162 --> 00:26:10,586
At first he went easily.

566
00:26:10,606 --> 00:26:12,693
The food had added to his strength.

567
00:26:12,713 --> 00:26:16,363
But it had become very hot, and sleep
began to bend him down;

568
00:26:16,383 --> 00:26:20,576
yet he kept going, thinking — endure for
an hour, live for a lifetime.

569
00:26:21,346 --> 00:26:25,963
He went on a good way on this side too,
and was just about to turn left,

570
00:26:25,983 --> 00:26:30,536
when look — a damp hollow came up; he was
sorry to leave it. He thinks:

571
00:26:30,856 --> 00:26:32,638
“Flax would come up well here.”

572
00:26:32,958 --> 00:26:34,289
Again he went straight.

573
00:26:34,309 --> 00:26:37,343
He took in the hollow, dug a hole beyond
the hollow,

574
00:26:37,363 --> 00:26:39,046
and turned the second corner.

575
00:26:39,066 --> 00:26:43,040
Pakhom looked back at the shikhan: from
the heat it had misted over,

576
00:26:43,060 --> 00:26:44,743
something wavered in the air,

577
00:26:44,763 --> 00:26:48,795
and through the haze the people on the
shikhan could barely be seen —

578
00:26:48,815 --> 00:26:50,851
it would be fifteen versts to them.

579
00:26:51,171 --> 00:26:51,828
“Well,”

580
00:26:52,148 --> 00:26:53,361
thinks Pakhom,

581
00:26:53,681 --> 00:26:57,372
“I have taken long sides; I must make this
one shorter.”

582
00:26:57,692 --> 00:27:01,236
He went along the third side, began to
quicken his step.

583
00:27:01,256 --> 00:27:02,636
He looked at the sun —

584
00:27:02,656 --> 00:27:06,199
it was already coming to the time of the
afternoon meal,

585
00:27:06,219 --> 00:27:09,827
and on the third side he had gone only two
versts in all.

586
00:27:09,847 --> 00:27:13,263
And to the place it was still the same
fifteen versts.

587
00:27:13,583 --> 00:27:14,236
“No,”

588
00:27:14,556 --> 00:27:15,420
he thinks,

589
00:27:15,740 --> 00:27:19,836
“even if the plot is crooked, I must make
straight for it in time.

590
00:27:19,856 --> 00:27:21,395
I must not take too much.

591
00:27:21,415 --> 00:27:24,077
And there is already a great deal of
land.”

592
00:27:24,397 --> 00:27:28,368
Pakhom dug a hole quickly and turned
straight toward the shikhan.

593
00:27:30,888 --> 00:27:35,419
Pakhom goes straight toward the shikhan,
and it was already hard for him.

594
00:27:35,439 --> 00:27:38,973
He was in a sweat, and had cut and bruised
his bare feet,

595
00:27:38,993 --> 00:27:40,905
and his legs began to give way.

596
00:27:40,925 --> 00:27:43,524
He longed to rest, but it was impossible —

597
00:27:43,544 --> 00:27:45,831
he would not get there before sunset.

598
00:27:45,851 --> 00:27:48,636
The sun does not wait; it goes down and
down.

599
00:27:48,956 --> 00:27:49,609
“Ah,”

600
00:27:49,929 --> 00:27:50,793
he thinks,

601
00:27:51,113 --> 00:27:54,624
“have I not made a mistake, have I not
taken too much?

602
00:27:54,644 --> 00:27:56,324
What if I am not in time?”

603
00:27:56,644 --> 00:28:01,224
He looks ahead at the shikhan, he looks at
the sun: the place is far off,

604
00:28:01,244 --> 00:28:04,059
and the sun is already not far from the
edge.

605
00:28:04,829 --> 00:28:07,088
So Pakhom goes, it is hard for him,

606
00:28:07,108 --> 00:28:09,952
and still he quickens and quickens his
step.

607
00:28:09,972 --> 00:28:13,728
He went and went — it was still far; he
broke into a trot.

608
00:28:13,748 --> 00:28:18,090
He threw away his jacket, his boots, the
flask, threw away his cap,

609
00:28:18,110 --> 00:28:20,499
only held the spade and leaned on it.

610
00:28:20,819 --> 00:28:21,473
“Ah,”

611
00:28:21,793 --> 00:28:22,656
he thinks,

612
00:28:22,976 --> 00:28:25,514
“I was greedy, I have ruined everything,

613
00:28:25,534 --> 00:28:27,880
I shall not run there before sunset.”

614
00:28:28,200 --> 00:28:31,837
And fear made it still worse for him to
catch his breath.

615
00:28:31,857 --> 00:28:36,008
Pakhom runs, his shirt and trousers stick
to his body with sweat,

616
00:28:36,028 --> 00:28:37,099
his mouth is dry.

617
00:28:37,119 --> 00:28:39,986
His chest swells like a blacksmith’s
bellows,

618
00:28:40,006 --> 00:28:44,285
and his heart beats like a hammer, and his
legs, as if not his own,

619
00:28:44,305 --> 00:28:47,172
give way. Pakhom grew frightened and
thought:

620
00:28:47,492 --> 00:28:49,023
“I may die of the strain.”

621
00:28:49,793 --> 00:28:52,485
He is afraid of dying, but cannot stop.

622
00:28:52,805 --> 00:28:54,403
“After running so far,”

623
00:28:54,723 --> 00:28:55,587
he thinks,

624
00:28:55,907 --> 00:28:58,361
“to stop now — they will call me a fool.”

625
00:28:58,681 --> 00:29:01,054
He ran and ran, was coming close now,

626
00:29:01,074 --> 00:29:04,483
and hears the Bashkirs shrieking and
shouting at him,

627
00:29:04,503 --> 00:29:07,653
and at their shouting his heart burns
still more.

628
00:29:07,673 --> 00:29:09,917
Pakhom runs with his last strength,

629
00:29:09,937 --> 00:29:14,380
and the sun is already coming to the edge,
it has gone into the mist:

630
00:29:14,400 --> 00:29:15,610
large, red, bloody.

631
00:29:15,630 --> 00:29:17,680
Any moment it will begin to set.

632
00:29:17,700 --> 00:29:20,979
The sun is near, and the place is not very
far now.

633
00:29:20,999 --> 00:29:25,572
Pakhom already sees the people on the
shikhan waving their arms at him,

634
00:29:25,592 --> 00:29:26,477
urging him on.

635
00:29:26,497 --> 00:29:30,229
He sees the fox-fur cap on the ground and
the money on it;

636
00:29:30,249 --> 00:29:33,140
he sees the elder too, sitting on the
ground,

637
00:29:33,160 --> 00:29:35,275
holding his belly with his hands.

638
00:29:35,295 --> 00:29:37,345
And Pakhom remembered the dream.

639
00:29:37,665 --> 00:29:39,425
“There is a great deal of land,”

640
00:29:39,745 --> 00:29:40,608
he thinks,

641
00:29:40,928 --> 00:29:43,283
“but will God bring me to live on it?

642
00:29:43,303 --> 00:29:44,952
Oh, I have ruined myself,”

643
00:29:45,272 --> 00:29:46,136
he thinks,

644
00:29:46,456 --> 00:29:47,929
“I shall not run there.”

645
00:29:48,699 --> 00:29:51,977
Pakhom looked at the sun, and it had
reached the earth,

646
00:29:51,997 --> 00:29:54,796
was already beginning to go under with its
rim,

647
00:29:54,816 --> 00:29:57,075
and was cut out in an arc at the edge.

648
00:29:57,095 --> 00:30:01,092
Pakhom put on speed with his last
strength, threw his body forward,

649
00:30:01,112 --> 00:30:04,930
his legs barely managing to catch him so
that he would not fall.

650
00:30:04,950 --> 00:30:08,289
Pakhom ran up to the shikhan, and suddenly
it grew dark.

651
00:30:08,309 --> 00:30:10,747
He looked back — the sun had already set.

652
00:30:10,767 --> 00:30:11,587
Pakhom gasped.

653
00:30:11,907 --> 00:30:13,348
“My labours are lost,”

654
00:30:13,668 --> 00:30:17,586
he thinks. He was about to stop, but hears
the Bashkirs still shouting,

655
00:30:17,606 --> 00:30:21,358
and remembered that from below it seems to
him that the sun has set,

656
00:30:21,378 --> 00:30:23,854
but from the shikhan the sun has not yet
set.

657
00:30:23,874 --> 00:30:26,683
Pakhom drew himself up and ran up onto the
shikhan.

658
00:30:26,703 --> 00:30:28,569
On the shikhan it was still light.

659
00:30:28,589 --> 00:30:30,122
Pakhom ran up, sees the cap.

660
00:30:30,142 --> 00:30:32,396
Before the cap sits the elder, guffawing,

661
00:30:32,416 --> 00:30:34,226
holding his belly with his hands.

662
00:30:34,246 --> 00:30:37,277
Pakhom remembered the dream, gasped, his
legs gave way,

663
00:30:37,297 --> 00:30:40,217
and he fell forward, reaching the cap with
his hands.

664
00:30:40,987 --> 00:30:42,331
“Ah, well done!”

665
00:30:42,651 --> 00:30:43,810
cried the elder.

666
00:30:44,130 --> 00:30:46,394
“You have taken a great deal of land!”

667
00:30:47,164 --> 00:30:50,128
Pakhom’s hired man ran up, wanted to lift
him,

668
00:30:50,148 --> 00:30:53,631
but blood was running from his mouth, and
he lay dead.

669
00:30:54,401 --> 00:30:57,413
The Bashkirs clicked their tongues and
pitied him.

670
00:30:58,183 --> 00:31:01,972
The hired man picked up the spade, dug a
grave for Pakhom —

671
00:31:01,992 --> 00:31:05,909
exactly as much as he had covered from his
feet to his head —

672
00:31:05,929 --> 00:31:07,846
three arshins, and buried him.
